WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.950
Narrator: This video will show active learning
using worksheets in a large interactive physics

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:11.950
class.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.980
It will discuss why and how to use worksheets
in lecture.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:22.020
The class is one section of an algebra-based
introductory course that has typically 750

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:27.029
students enrolled in three sections.

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:31.750
When entering the lecture hall, the students
pick up a worksheet that contains most of

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.450
the activities for the lecture.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:41.250
In this example, the worksheet is designed
to support an understanding of electric circuits.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.580
The class starts with a relatively brief introduction.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.550
The students are reminded of what has been
discussed so far and some context for the

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:50.550
class is provided.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:55.870
Instructor: You remember this example from
last class with the two identical bulbs and

00:00:55.870 --> 00:01:00.400
there was a switch across one of the light
bulbs, across bulb B

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.830
Narrator: The introduction ends with a motivation
for the first worksheet task and then students

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:10.221
are directed to start working on the first
worksheet problem

00:01:10.221 --> 00:01:17.810
Instructor: Use the definition of power
and our circuit rules to predict what happens

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.870
when this switch

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:24.620
Narrator: Students are typically given 7-8
minutes to work, during which they are encouraged

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:27.420
to discuss solution strategies with their
peers.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:32.550
Instructor: In my approach, the worksheets
are actually the second exposure of the students

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.440
to the new material.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:39.570
The first exposure to new content happens
in the first pre-class reading assignment.

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:47.720
I tell my students that I expect them only
to know the basic definitions and perhaps

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:54.170
to understand the basic concepts, without
really expecting them to know everything in

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:57.660
depth at this point.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:05.060
Typical worksheets contain conceptual questions
they contain relatively difficult problem

00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:12.120
questions that where the students have to
use some important procedural knowledge such

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:21.630
as free-body diagrams, or graphs, or circuit
diagrams - thigs like that.

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:29.890
In contrast to worked problems where I just
go through the solution, the worksheet activities

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:35.800
give the students time to work on the problems
in class at their own pace.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:42.380
Lecture TA and I visit with student groups,
we will look at their worksheets and see where

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:47.100
students get stuck, we will ask them where
they have difficulties if for instance,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:52.090
several student groups have the same difficulties,
we can interrupt the activity and give hints

00:02:52.090 --> 00:02:54.270
to the entire class.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:59.610
Narrator: Having a teaching assistant is
valuable in such a large class.

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:06.250
The lecture TA answers student questions during
worksheet activities and after class.

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:11.900
He/She can alert the instructor to technical
problems, such as when the writing is too

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.410
small or the microphone not loud enough.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:22.190
He/She can carry a microphone and repeat student
questions the instructor missed, or let the

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:27.790
instructor know when a solution is not clear
and needs more explanation.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:33.460
The lecture TA can also give important feedback
after class and tell the instructor which

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:39.810
activity motivated the students and which
things are now clear or still unclear to the

00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:41.040
students.

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:50.760
Instructor: I give the students roughly
7-8 minutes, based on experience, for a worksheet

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:56.599
problem, and it turns out that in that time,
roughly two-thirds of the students will have

00:03:56.599 --> 00:03:59.769
finished the activity.

00:03:59.769 --> 00:04:03.710
I often use clicker questions to help me with
the timing.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:11.989
So, for instance, I ask students to answer
a follow-up clicker question, and from the

00:04:11.989 --> 00:04:18.500
numbers the clicker shows me, I can tell how
many students have answered the question.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:23.430
So I aim for roughly two-thirds of the class
to finish.

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:29.620
Narrator: Worksheet tasks should be difficult
enough to encourage peer discussions.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:34.000
There is little value in having worksheet
problems that the students can quickly solve

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:35.340
on their own.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:41.669
Instructor: Because not all students are
able to finish, there is of course the concern

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:43.960
that the weaker students become disengaged.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:51.250
However, I often tell my students that they
are not expected to finish a solution that

00:04:51.250 --> 00:05:00.430
it is important just to think very hard about
how to approach a problem and that way they

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:07.720
have thought about a problem enough so that
when I show the solution, they can compare

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.090
their approach to mine.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:14.840
So, even if they don't finish, they get a
lot out of the worksheet activities.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:21.240
Narrator: Asking a closely related clicker
question is a good way to initiate the follow-up

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.490
and bring the attention back to the instructor.

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:29.410
Instructor: ...or is A and B smaller than
C and D, perhaps take another minute and

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:34.910
discuss this with your neighbors that disagree
with your answer and see if you can convince

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:36.460
your neighbors.

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:42.710
Narrator: The clicker question and peer
discussion usually takes 3-5 minutes.

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:49.040
A detailed solution with discussion follows.

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.210
That can take up to 10 minutes.

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:58.490
So, a complete worksheet activity with follow-up
can take anywhere between 10 and 30 minutes

00:05:58.490 --> 00:06:02.770
depending on complexity and difficulty.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:08.320
In the 80-minute physics class, 4 worksheet
problems are discussed on average.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:17.030
Instructor: [...] I2 is equal to I3 and
so are these greater or smaller than I1?

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:22.080
Instructor: The follow-up is an essential
part of a worksheet activity.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:28.430
The students had spent some time thinking
hard about a solution and they want to know

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:33.570
now if their way, their approach, is the right
one.

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:39.240
So in order for them to be able to follow
my solution and to compare their thoughts,

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:47.009
I need to go over the solution in detailed
steps, and I need to take my time with it.

00:06:47.009 --> 00:06:53.740
So I usually write on a document camera when
I do this, and when I explain rather than

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:56.940
showing the solution just on a powerpoint
slide.

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:02.490
I use powerpoint only for introductions and
summaries

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:04.380
Instructor (to class): right?

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:11.389
So the last steps are I can do it with you
here so you know that, let's say you get

00:07:11.389 --> 00:07:17.380
Instructor: I don't grade my worksheets, some
instructors do, but in any case it is very

00:07:17.380 --> 00:07:20.639
important that students get to keep their
worksheets.

00:07:20.639 --> 00:07:26.289
The students use them as important student
tools because the worksheets contain the most

00:07:26.289 --> 00:07:29.040
important activities for each lecture.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.240
Narrator: Worksheets are a great way to
increase student learning in lecture.

00:07:34.240 --> 00:07:39.740
They allow students to work at their own pace
and allow the instructor to monitor students'

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:44.710
progress and provide immediate feedback and
help.

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:48.820
Worksheets are not difficult to design and
are much appreciated by students.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:49.820
[Instrumental music]

